free a texture, rather than parking it on a list to be recycled

There was no way to give a texture's memory back. A shed one went onto
CartFaces' free list and was reused by update_texture when something else
wanted a texture, so everything freed today -- nine cart faces instead of
eighteen, one while a game plays -- reduced how many were ever *created* and
returned nothing to the system. That is why each fix moved the deadline
instead of removing it.

Measured on the handheld, which is what finally made the shape plain:

    shelf, no core loaded   RSS 44 MB   anon 27 MB   heap 16.9 MB
    playing, gpSP loaded    RSS 78.5    anon 61.4    heap 16.9

78.5 MB of a device with 101 MB and about 20 MB of system. gpSP's 34 MB is
mostly irreducible -- 8 MB of ROM and the JIT cache that makes it fast -- and
slot's 44 MB is almost all art that is not on the screen while a game is.

So: `free_texture` on both backends, emptied slots reused by the next create
rather than growing the table, and two callers to start with. The shelf's two
full-screen grounds and the user's wallpaper, 1.68 MB each, are handed back
while a game is on the screen and rebuilt by the paths that built them. The
cart faces shed on launch are freed for real rather than recycled, since in
play nothing else is going to want them.

Drawing with a freed id is a miss rather than a panic, which the frontend
needs for the frame between freeing the art and rebuilding it, and there is a
test for exactly that.

The cost is a beat of plain background and placeholder carts on the way back
to the shelf. That was looked at on the handheld rather than argued about,
and the verdict was "ejecting to the menu feels fine" -- so the rest of the
shelf's art can follow the same way when it is wanted: the 62 cart title
faces, the chrome, the GBA cart art, another 5-8 MB that is not on screen
while a game is.

The two carousel grounds were also kept as decoded RGBA in a OnceLock for the
life of the process -- 1.68 MB each, beside the textures made from them, for
pictures that are not on the screen while a game is. They are decoded on
demand now and dropped after upload, which is 3.4 MB of heap on top of the
8 MB of textures, and costs a decode when the shelf comes back: the same beat
the freed textures already pay for.

